<UserControl x:Class="RPM.Desktop.Presentation.Startup.SplashCard"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:controls="using:RPM.Desktop.Presentation.Controls"
             RequestedTheme="Dark">

  <!-- Transparent all the way down. On Windows the window itself is clear (see SplashWindow.Clear),
       so the mark stands on the desktop, and a background painted here would cover it. On macOS the
       window is cut to the wordmark's shape instead, and the card paints the Ground under it
       (FillForCut).

       No edge either, unless the card falls back to glass (DrawEdge): a window that cannot be made
       clear gets desktop acrylic and the edge the app's own cards have, one pixel of #2EFFFFFF. The
       edge is drawn here and not by the compositor: SplashWindow asks DWM to draw no line at all
       (WindowEdges.Round), so this is the only one.

       CornerRadius 8 is the radius that Windows 11 gives a window. It must match: the window is
       clipped to that shape whatever this says, so a larger radius here has its corners cut off and a
       smaller one leaves a sliver of glass outside the line. -->
  <Border x:Name="Edge" Background="Transparent" CornerRadius="8" BorderBrush="Transparent" BorderThickness="1">
    <Grid Background="Transparent">

    <!-- The ground for a window cut to the wordmark's shape (the macOS splash, see FillForCut). Only
         the inside of the letters is on screen, so this is seen as the dimmed letters, and the band
         lights them from above. The colour is the TV's dim level, 0.32 white, on black, and it is
         opaque: the Skia surface on macOS cannot be seen through (see MacWindowCard). -->
    <Border x:Name="Ground" Background="#FF525252" Visibility="Collapsed"/>

    <!-- The card's own glass, for a head that cannot use the system's.
         It is collapsed by default: on Windows the glass is desktop acrylic behind the window, and
         anything painted here would cover it. macOS shows it when its window cannot be cut to the
         wordmark. See SplashWindow.Show on that head and MacWindowCard, which explains why a
         see-through Skia surface is not possible.

         What it imitates: the Windows card is DesktopAcrylicKind.Thin in dark theme. That is a light,
         neutral, translucent pane with the desktop's own colour showing through. So the plate uses
         neutral greys, fairly light, and no accent colour.

         What it cannot do is show the desktop (see MacWindowCard). So it is a fixed pane where Windows
         has a live one. The grey values below are the numbers to adjust if the two heads do not look
         the same: lighter is closer to thin acrylic over a bright desktop, darker is closer to it over
         a dark one. -->
    <Grid x:Name="Plate" Visibility="Collapsed">
      <Border CornerRadius="7">
        <Border.Background>
          <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
            <GradientStop Color="#FF3C4045" Offset="0"/>
            <GradientStop Color="#FF2E3237" Offset="1"/>
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </Border.Background>
      </Border>

      <!-- The light along the top, which makes a flat fill look like a pane of glass. It is fairly
           strong: a bright ground needs a bright highlight to look like the same pane, and thin
           acrylic has a clear highlight at its top edge. -->
      <Border CornerRadius="7">
        <Border.Background>
          <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
            <GradientStop Color="#2BFFFFFF" Offset="0"/>
            <GradientStop Color="#00FFFFFF" Offset="0.55"/>
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </Border.Background>
      </Border>
    </Grid>

    <!-- The Android TV app's opening: the wordmark dimmed, with a band of light sweeping across it
         once a second for as long as the card is up (see SweptMark). It is the card's only content,
         centred and filling it: 252 wide, 24 in from either side, at the wordmark's own 2.6 to 1.

         It is vector Paths and not the SVG asset, because an Image decodes asynchronously and this
         window must be on screen while the app has nothing else to show. -->
    <controls:SweptMark x:Name="Mark" Width="252" Height="97"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
    </Grid>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
